{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Object Oriented Programming Principles"}, {"type": "position", "content": 3}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 1}, {"type": "level", "content": "A1.2"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you use when talking about OOP principles."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Describe common simple incidents at work in a simple spoken or written situation with a supportive interlocutor. Build the macrosystem conceptual model following the customer requirements, through the use and interpretation of collected information, represented in class diagrams, interaction diagrams, and operation contracts."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Principles in OOP: abstraction, encapsulation, generalization/specialization, polymorphism. Communicates effectively in English about the communicative topics for this level through the use of appropriate basic vocabulary and grammatical structures, and with intelligible pronunciation."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe8n2le01ob03re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Review of Demonstrative Pronouns in Affirmative Statements"}, {"type": "describer", "content": " You use <span class=\"strong\">demonstrative pronouns</span> to point at or identify specific things within a sentence."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"strong\">Demonstrative pronouns</span> can be singular or plural.</br></br>  You use <span class=\"strong\">demonstrative pronouns</span> depending on how near or far is the thing you want to point or identify."}, {"type": "table", "content": "{\"rows\":3,\"columns\":3,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Singular\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Plural\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"strong\\\">Near</span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"This\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"These\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"strong\\\">Far</span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"That\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Those\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" intro_table_border instruction_table_background\"}"}, {"type": "example", "content": "I want you to analyze this graphic."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Surely, you want to know those main aspects about interfaces or software security, but you don't need to know how the screen works."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Verbs With Infinitives in Affirmative Statements With  'Want To'"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You use <span class=\"strong\">verbs</span>  and <span class=\"strong\">infinitives</span>  in <span class=\"strong\">affirmative</span>  statements with <span class=\"strong\">want to</span>  to express that you desire or wish something."}, {"type": "text", "content": "You need to be careful when writing a<span class=\"strong\"> verb</span> and an <span class=\"strong\">infinitive</span> because you don't use <span class=\"strong\">to</span> as a common preposition. <br /><br />  You use the following structure when writing a <span class=\"strong\">verb</span> and <span class=\"strong\">infinitive</span> in affirmative statements with <span class=\"strong\">want to</span>. <br /><br />  <div class=\"center_text\">Subject + <span class=\"strong\">Want to</span>+ Verb in infinitive</div><br /><br />   Example:<br />  <span class=\"italic\"> I <span class=\"strong\">want to add</span> specific data to the system.</span>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "I want to inform you about those main OOP principles."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Let's pretend you want to change not only your operating system but your computer. "}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Understanding Abstraction in OOP"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You use <span class=\"strong\">abstraction</span> when you want to consider some complex ideas, but you don't want to focus on irrelevant details. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Let's imagine the following situation: <br /><br />  You want to swap your personal computer for a week with a friend. You probably want to give him some essential information about your computer such as what operating system it uses, what kind of battery charger it has, or maybe what your administration password is. <br /><br />  However, you don't want to give him irrelevant details about your computer such as the internet explorer you use or where to find the calculator. <br /><br />  Abstraction gives you only the important facts without telling you the irrelevant details about something."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "bogged down "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to be overload"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: This principle makes us consider relevant information about something without becoming bogged down in detail."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n2le01ob03aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "encapsulation "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "OOP principle that explains you how to operate something without knowing its technical parts"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: This fact represents the encapsulation principle because it allows you to know how something works without worrying about its technical details."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n2le01ob03aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "polymorphism "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "ability to interact with an object irrespective of its specialized category"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: The last principle is polymorphism. It doesn't matter what computer you want to buy because you know how to interact with it already."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n2le01ob03aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "desktop "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "computer you use on a desk"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: I want you to pretend you need to buy a new computer and you are going to choose between certain kinds of machines such as desktops or laptop computers."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n2le01ob03aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "inherit "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to have characteristics from the predecessor"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: This is specialization, a principle that establishes that machines inherit characteristics of the generalized category."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n2le01ob03aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Find out more about the OOP principles that you learned."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Create a conceptual map in which you explain the principles."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Share your conceptual map with your partner. "}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I research about OOP principles."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I design a conceptual map to explain the principles."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I share my conceptual map with others. "}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"template_override": 1, "type": "welcome", "_data": [], "contents": [{}], "id": 0}, {"_data": [], "title": "", "type": "audio", "template_override": 1, "id": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"audiosrc": "enpe8n2le01ob03re2aud01", "images": [{"time": "100", "image": "enpe8n2le01ob03re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<div align=\"justify\">Tonight, I want to inform you about those main OOP principles. I want you to analyze this graphic; it can help us to understand how these principles work. Abstraction: This principle makes us consider relevant information about something without becoming bogged down in detail; in other words, this principle helps us ignore irrelevant details. For example, if you want to get a new computer with a different operating system; surely, you want to know those main aspects about interfaces or software security, but you don't need to know how the screen works.</div>"}, {"time": "38000", "image": "enpe8n2le01ob03re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<div align=\"justify\">Now, let's pretend you want to change not only your operating system but your computer. To do this, you may want to know how to operate your new computer, thus without being versed about every single component of the computer. This fact represents the encapsulation principle because it allows you to know how something works without worrying about its technical details.</div>"}, {"time": "63000", "image": "enpe8n2le01ob03re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "<div align=\"justify\">Generalization and specialization are OOP principles that work together; I want you to pretend you need to buy a new computer and you are going to choose between certain kinds of machines such as desktops or laptop computers. This fact represents the generalization principle because computers are certainly generalized under the same category. Now, even when these objects are in the same category, they have specificities and different versions. This is specialization, a principle that establishes that machines inherit characteristics of the generalized category.\nThe last principle is polymorphism. It doesn't matter what computer you want to buy because you know how to interact with it already. Machines share common handling procedures, so you don't need special training to operate any types of computers.</div>"}], "coverimg": "enpe8n2le01ob03re2img01.jpg"}, "type": "slideshow"}]}], "exercises": [{"stimuli_ids": [0], "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can list the principles in OOP."}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can talk about the characteristics for principles in OOP"}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can discuss simple ideas in real life that relate to the OOP principles.  "}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "Why do you think there are different types of software?"}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "Would you like to know essential collections of software for managing programming packages? Why?"}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"type": "group_name", "content": "Welcome"}], "type": "group"}, {"type": "feedback", "content": {"attempt_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": "You have 2 tries"}, {"type": "text", "content": "you have 1 attempt left"}], "correct_feedback": "Correct", "incorrect_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": "try again"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Maybe next time"}], "allowed_attempts": 2}}, {"type": "free_header_text", "content": "Ask your partner:"}, {"type": "free_type_text_area", "content": "Principles in OOP"}], "questions": [], "type": "WLC", "template_override": 1, "id": 0, "img_path": ""}, {"stimuli_ids": [1], "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": " This professor wants to talk about <span class=\"tool-tip\" id=\"tip_1_0\">OOP </span>principles"}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"type": "group_name", "content": "Content"}], "type": "group"}, {"type": "feedback", "content": {"attempt_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": ""},